1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,105
Every attention-analytics product promises
to tell you

2
00:00:04,105 --> 00:00:07,450
which stretches lose your audience, and
why.

3
00:00:07,450 --> 00:00:12,540
We built that, tested the premise, and it
did not survive.

4
00:00:12,540 --> 00:00:14,200
This is Second Pass.

5
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:17,650
Not the predictor — the machine that
catches

6
00:00:17,650 --> 00:00:22,120
the predictor lying, pointed first at our
own hypothesis.

7
00:00:22,120 --> 00:00:25,370
Attention climbs toward the end of a
runtime.

8
00:00:25,370 --> 00:00:30,420
On Tears of Steel it looked like
four-point-four times the first half.

9
00:00:30,420 --> 00:00:33,210
Then we checked what was in those
segments.

10
00:00:33,210 --> 00:00:36,443
A fifth of this upload is end credits, and
the

11
00:00:36,443 --> 00:00:40,450
most replayed moment in the file is a
post-credits scene.

12
00:00:40,450 --> 00:00:43,300
On the film itself it is
one-point-two-eight.

13
00:00:43,300 --> 00:00:46,201
And the one correlation that used to clear

14
00:00:46,201 --> 00:00:50,000
our significance floor did not survive
taking them out.

15
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,679
Every number you just saw came out of
ClickHouse,

16
00:00:53,679 --> 00:00:58,260
through the official mcp-clickhouse MCP
server, on Cloud Run.

17
00:00:58,260 --> 00:01:01,250
The model does not write SQL.

18
00:01:01,250 --> 00:01:06,112
It picks one of seven parameterised
questions and fills in the arguments,

19
00:01:06,112 --> 00:01:11,040
so the statements stay deterministic —
which is why this demo is unedited.

20
00:01:11,040 --> 00:01:15,400
Here is how you would know if we were
fooling ourselves.

21
00:01:15,400 --> 00:01:20,900
The same query, every feature paired
against the wrong segment's attention.

22
00:01:20,900 --> 00:01:22,800
Everything collapses to nothing.

23
00:01:22,800 --> 00:01:26,651
A method that still found structure in
that would be

24
00:01:26,651 --> 00:01:30,280
manufacturing it — and it runs live, on a
toggle.

25
00:01:30,280 --> 00:01:35,670
Across three films the directions agree no
better than coin flips.

26
00:01:35,670 --> 00:01:41,870
One correlation clears its floor at all,
and the column itself calls it marginal.

27
00:01:41,870 --> 00:01:44,950
None of this is really about film.

28
00:01:44,950 --> 00:01:48,333
Every sequence has this confound in it —

29
00:01:48,333 --> 00:01:53,660
retention curves, funnels, onboarding, any
test read over time.

30
00:01:53,660 --> 00:01:57,948
Something always rises toward the end, and
correlating

31
00:01:57,948 --> 00:02:01,600
against it hands you a confident wrong
answer.

32
00:02:01,600 --> 00:02:04,258
So we pointed the same discipline at

33
00:02:04,258 --> 00:02:08,910
something completely different:
seventy-three real demo videos.

34
00:02:08,910 --> 00:02:13,843
Ask how long yours should be and it tells
you where you sit and

35
00:02:13,843 --> 00:02:19,010
how wide the spread is — then refuses to
say anything is too long.

36
00:02:19,010 --> 00:02:20,360
A median is not a rule.

37
00:02:20,360 --> 00:02:23,700
Four checks a dashboard does not run.

38
00:02:23,700 --> 00:02:25,290
Detrend before claiming.

39
00:02:25,290 --> 00:02:27,340
Correct for multiplicity.

40
00:02:27,340 --> 00:02:31,880
Shuffle the outcome and confirm the
finding dies.

41
00:02:31,880 --> 00:02:34,110
Test whether it replicates.

42
00:02:34,110 --> 00:02:37,400
The machine that catches the predictor
lying.

43
00:02:37,400 --> 00:02:39,560
It has caught us three times.

